## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a real-time monocular system integrating segmentation, 3D pose estimation with scene priors, grasp-point planning, and physics-based stability reasoning—plus efficient edge adaptation with minimal shots.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D pose estimation using environmental priors
- Grasp-point prediction in cluttered scenes
- Differentiable physics modules for stability evaluation
- On-device few-shot adaptation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for the model heads
function forward(img, shelfPrior) {
  const features = backbone(img);
  const segmentation = segmentationHead(features);
  const pose = poseHead(features, shelfPrior);
  const grasp = graspHead(features, pose);
  const stability = physicsPrior(grasp, pose);
  return { segmentation, pose, grasp, stability };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a complete, safety-critical computer vision pipeline operating under real-time constraints in dynamic, cluttered environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular 3D reconstruction leveraging temporal and geometric cues
- Multi-object tracking in scenarios with frequent clutter and occlusion
- Hazard prediction from partial observations and contact-event inference
- Few-shot on-device adaptation for novel object categories
- Edge-GPU optimization: latency, memory footprint, and compute budget trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: modules and data flow
class Monocular3DSystem {
  constructor() {
    this.depthNet = new LightweightDepthEncoder();
    this.tracker = new MultiObjectTracker();
    this.hazardPredictor = new ContactEventPredictor();
    this.adaptationHead = new FewShotAdapter();
  }

  processFrame(frame, context = {}) {
    const depth = this.depthNet.infer(frame);
    const poses = this.depthNet.extractEgoMotion(depth, context.prevDepth);
    const objects = this.tracker.associate(depth, poses);
    const hazards = this.hazardPredictor.predict(objects, depth, poses);
    return { depth, poses, objects, hazards };
  }

  adapt(shots) {
    return this.adaptationHead.fineTune(shots);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design cross-modal, real-time computer vision pipelines that integrate on-device adaptation with differentiable geometry for robotic manipulation and planning in unstructured environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth and surface normals estimation from a single RGB camera
- Differentiable probabilistic mesh prediction integrated with motion planning
- Few-shot (5-shot) on-device adaptation using synthetic pre-training followed by real-world fine-tuning
- Multi-task learning with shared encoders and task-specific heads

## Code Example

